"""Preparation-time reference for the in-place ``renorm_`` operator (849).

Frozen Definition ``definitions/renorm_.json``:

    aten::renorm_(Tensor(a!) self, Scalar p, int dim, Scalar maxnorm) -> Tensor(a!)
    effects.mutates == ["self"]
    effects.returns_alias_of == {"out": "self"}

So the callable has to renormalize the caller's storage in place and return the
very same Tensor object: no new storage, no copy-out, no dtype/device change.

The oracle delegates to the native ATen kernel that the frozen master tests
already use on their expected side (``tests/test_renorm_.py`` and
``tests/test_renorm.py::test_renorm_`` both call ``torch.ops.aten.renorm_`` on a
cloned ``to_reference`` input).  This module imports no FlagGems symbol and
never looks up ``flag_gems.renorm_``, so the injected candidate can never serve
as its own oracle.  ``aten.renorm_`` is only dispatch-patched inside
``flag_gems.use_gems()``/``flag_gems.enable()``; the target tests select
``-m renorm_`` and call the public wrapper directly, so calling the native
kernel here does not re-enter the overridden export.
"""

import torch

__all__ = ["run", "renorm_"]


def run(self, p, dim, maxnorm):
    """Renormalize ``self`` along ``dim`` in place and return the same tensor."""
    torch.ops.aten.renorm_(self, p, dim, maxnorm)
    # ``aten.renorm_`` writes into ``self`` and returns it; returning the input
    # object explicitly keeps the frozen alias/identity contract observable
    # (``res is inp`` and an unchanged ``data_ptr()``).
    return self


# Same callable under the operator name, so an override spec without an
# explicit function name (``renorm_:<file>``) still resolves to this
# implementation while the code object name stays the declared entrypoint.
renorm_ = run
